    "summary": "High‑energy show with monster Caution→Uncle John’s jam and soaring China Cat",
    "blurb": "The March 22, 1972 Academy of Music concert delivers a blazing second set highlighted by the extended Sugar Magnolia > Caution > Jam > Uncle John's Band sequence. Jerry's lead work shines on Mr. Charlie and the China Cat Sunflower → I Know You Rider pair, while Keith adds lush organ textures throughout. Overall a strong performance despite some early‑set technical hiccups.",
    "review": "What unfolds at the Academy of Music on March 22, 1972 finds the Dead firing on all cylinders. After a rough opening that sees Deal wobble and monitor issues on Loser, the band quickly regains its footing with a stellar Mr. Charlie where Garcia’s guitar is both crisp and adventurous, even flirting with a volume‑pedal flourish. The classic China Cat Sunflower → I Know You Rider transition soars, laying down a bright, melodic runway for the rest of the night.\n\nThe second set is where this show truly shines: Sugar Magnolia launches into an inspired Caution (Do Not Stop On Tracks) that blossoms into an expansive jam before resolving into Uncle John's Band. The energy never wanes, and Keith’s organ/piano work adds depth to the improvisations, while Bill Kreutzmann drives the group forward with a propulsive drum feel—though his mix sits a bit forward in the Miller soundboard. Highlights also include the haunting beauty of Looks Like Rain, the tender “Two Souls” moment (The Stranger), and an unforgettable Wharf Rat captured on a lo‑fi audience tape that still cuts through with emotional power. The encore, One More Saturday Night, caps the night with a joyous burst of classic Dead fun.",
